From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Elena Maher <12779416+elenaOnline@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 03:36:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] gpui: Add an app-store feature that builds macOS without
 private API

App Review rejects an app whose binary uses private API (guideline 2.5.1),
and a sandboxed store app may not start other processes (2.4.5). GPUI's
macOS backend does both: its windows override the private
_opaqueRectForWindowMoveWhenInTitlebar and, on their state unarchiver,
_windowRestorationOptions (which looks up the private class
NSWindowRestorationOptions by name); a titlebar double-click set to Fill
sends the private _zoomFill:; the diagonal resize cursors are NSCursor's
private _windowResizeNorthWestSouthEastCursor and
_windowResizeNorthEastSouthWestCursor; a blurred window background hides
AppKit's private CAChameleonLayer, strips a private CAFilter and clears
the backgrounds of the view's private sublayers; restart() runs /bin/bash
and open_with_system() runs `open`, which links fork, execvp and
posix_spawnp into every app. Review scans binaries for these names,
whether or not the code runs. GPUI also writes two undocumented defaults:
it registers NSWindowRestoresWorkspaceAtLaunch (restoring a window before
macOS 15) and sets NSAutoFillHeuristicControllerEnabled as the app
launches.

gpui_macos gets an `app-store` feature (and gpui_platform one that enables
it) that compiles all of that out:

- The diagonal resize cursors come from the public
  +[NSCursor frameResizeCursorFromPosition:inDirections:] (macOS 15);
  earlier systems, which have no public diagonal resize cursor, get the
  crosshair.
- open_with_system asks NSWorkspace to open the file URL, as `open` does,
  on a background thread as before.
- restart logs an error and does nothing: relaunching once this process
  has exited takes a helper process.
- Fill zooms with zoom:, without the system's tiling margins.
- Window state restores with a plain NSKeyedUnarchiver: the frame comes
  back, and the window opens on the current Space rather than its own.
- AppKit drags windows from the titlebar as usual, including windows that
  set app_owns_titlebar_drag (and it may delay their titlebar clicks, as
  it does on macOS 27).
- The blurred background clears only the view's own layer, and leaves
  NSVisualEffectView to draw its sublayers as it does.
- Neither default is written (the app may set the autofill one itself).

WindowOptions::app_owns_titlebar_drag and App::restart say what the
feature does to them. GPUI still reads the global defaults
AppleActionOnDoubleClick (a titlebar double-click's action),
AppleWindowTabbingMode and AppleFontSmoothing, with or without the
feature.

Without the feature nothing changes: the existing code only gains cfg
attributes (the two gated blocks re-indented), and the unarchiver class is
picked by a cfg-gated `let`; everything else is compiled only with the
feature.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
